\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Depth-graduated data curation.}
Predicted depth can serve as a continuous quality proxy for importance weighting in curation pipelines \cite{dsir2023,qurating2024}, retaining shallow-generation text while down-weighting deeply recursive samples that binary filters \cite{drayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse} would indiscriminately discard.

\paragraph{Deployment considerations.}
The temperature-induced d1/d2 AUC inversion (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}) means deploying without knowing the target decoding strategy risks systematic prediction reversals; strategy-conditioned classifiers or automatic strategy identification are needed.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced recursive generation depth estimation and showed that 19 lightweight statistical features---with p90 perplexity and surprisal variance as universal markers---achieve 70--85\% four-class accuracy (chance = 25\%) with $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$.
Cross-model transfer reaches 66.4\% and cross-strategy 59.1\%, though the temperature-induced d1/d2 inversion and PLMs' ${\sim}$59\% ceiling underscore remaining challenges; future work should address wild-data transfer, larger scales, and strategy-conditioned classifiers.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Cross-strategy deployment constraint.}
Deploying depth estimators across decoding strategies requires knowing the target strategy type a priori.
Without this knowledge, d1/d2 predictions may systematically invert: cross-strategy holdout d1v2 AUC drops to 0.129 (GPT-2~XL) and 0.253 (OLMo) when temperature-sampled text is classified by a model trained on nucleus and top-$k$ (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}).
Strategy-conditioned classifiers or automatic strategy identification are needed before deployment.

\paragraph{d0 inflation of headline metrics.}
Depth~0 (human-written text) is near-perfectly separable (recall $>$98\% across all settings), inflating headline accuracy.
Cross-model transfer accuracy of 66.4\% is substantially driven by d0; d2 recall drops to 26.8\% in the worst case (Pythia holdout).

\paragraph{Controlled vs.\ ecological setting.}
All experiments use Wikipedia seed text with controlled recursive generation (LoRA fine-tuning, 1--1.5B parameter models).
Transfer to mixed-domain corpora, instruction-tuned models, and larger scales (7B+) remains open.
Wild-data calibration is probed only indirectly; ecological transfer is future work.

\paragraph{EDD threshold sensitivity.}
The Effective Discriminable Depth depends on the AUC threshold $T$: at $T{=}0.80$, several cells lose one discriminable depth level relative to $T{=}0.75$.
The choice of $T$ is application-dependent and affects reported EDD values.

\paragraph{Reference model dependency.}
Surprisal-based features are computed relative to GPT-2 base (124M), introducing partial overlap when GPT-2~XL is held out, though GPT-2~XL holdout accuracy (67.6\%) does not exceed the unrelated OLMo (69.2\%).
The choice of reference model affects feature values; a different reference model may shift absolute feature magnitudes and potentially alter discriminability at depth boundaries.

\paragraph{Additional caveats.}
Detection features degrade under domain shift \cite{pudasaini2026_detection_fails}; our depth features are likely susceptible.
\citet{bakshi2026_collapse_improvement} find that early recursion can temporarily \emph{improve} model quality, potentially violating the monotonicity assumption, and \citet{falahati2026_curated} show that curated synthetic data need not trigger collapse, attenuating the fingerprints we exploit.

\section{Additional Results}
\label{app:additional}

\subsection{Top-$k$ Feature Trajectories}
\label{app:topk_trajectories}

Figure~\ref{fig:feature_trajectory} in the main text shows feature trajectories for nucleus ($p{=}0.95$) and temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) sampling.
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) trajectories follow the same monotonically decreasing trend for all features but with compressed inter-depth spacing, consistent with the signal attenuation effect discussed in \S\ref{sec:atlas:trajectories}.
Concretely, the d0$\to$d1 drop in \textsc{p90\_ppl} under top-$k$ is 78\% (vs.\ 87\% for nucleus), and the d2$\to$d3 reduction is 31\% (vs.\ 39\% for nucleus).
Despite the magnitude reduction, feature rank ordering across depths is preserved, which explains why ordinal classifiers maintain directionality even under top-$k$ conditions.

\subsection{Permutation Importance}
\label{app:permutation}

To verify that the Gini-based importance rankings in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features} are not an artifact of the impurity measure, we compute permutation importance (5-fold CV, 10 repeats) for all 9 atlas cells.
Results are highly consistent: \textsc{p90\_ppl} ranks in the top 2 in 9/9 cells under both measures, and \textsc{var\_surprisal} in 8/9 (vs.\ 7/9 for Gini, with the additional cell being OLMo top-$k$).
The mean Spearman rank correlation between Gini and permutation importance vectors across all 9 cells is $\rho{=}0.94$, confirming that the reported feature universality is robust to the choice of importance measure.

\subsection{DCOL Iteration Details}
\label{app:dcol_iterations}

Table~\ref{tab:dcol_iterations} summarizes all six DCOL iterations.
DeBERTa-v3-large exhibited systematic numerical instability across five configurations; RoBERTa-large resolved stability but could not close the accuracy gap with feature-based methods.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{DCOL iteration summary. All experiments use Pythia-1.4B nucleus data. RF baseline: 78.2\%.}
\label{tab:dcol_iterations}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Ver. & Backbone & Config & Outcome \\
\midrule
v1 & DeBERTa-large & Full FT, margin\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-3}$ & Embed.\ collapse (acc 31.5\%) \\
v2 & DeBERTa-large & Full FT, margin\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-4}$ & OOM (GPU contention) \\
v3 & DeBERTa-large & Full FT, margin\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-4}$ & Forgetting $+$ OOM \\
v4 & DeBERTa-large & LoRA $r{=}16$, m\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-3}$ & Embed.\ collapse (v1 repeat) \\
v5 & DeBERTa-large & LoRA $r{=}16$, m\_lr$\,{=}\,10^{-4}$ & NaN gradient explosion \\
v6 & RoBERTa-large & Full FT, grad clip 1.0 & Stable; acc 64.5\% ($-$13.7pp) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Feature Overlap Analysis}
\label{app:feature_overlap}

We probe whether end-to-end PLM representations capture the same depth signals as our statistical features.
DeBERTa attention importance and RF permutation importance exhibit a significant negative correlation (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.484$, $p{=}0.036$; top-5 Jaccard overlap$\,{=}\,0$).
PLM representations excel at surface lexical features (TTR: $R^2{=}0.85$, repeated bigrams) but fail to capture perplexity distribution statistics---\textsc{var\_surprisal} and \textsc{p90\_ppl} yield negative $R^2$ under linear probing.
This confirms that the 19pp accuracy gap between feature-based and end-to-end methods (\S\ref{sec:gen:dcol}) stems from complementary---not redundant---representations: PLMs capture lexical patterns while the statistical features capture distributional tail properties that constitute the dominant depth markers.
